The per-directory seam is put to a root stanza and two nested `(subdir ...)` groups. The
checker plugged into it contains no traversal of its own.

a newly plugged-in check receives the root and every nested directory without adding descent: true
an escaping source_tree dependency identifies a repository-wide scan: true
a local source_tree fixture does not identify a repository-wide scan: true

The refusal relationship is put to a synthesized `Verdict.fail` format. Its stable fragment
appears in no permanent control golden in the negative arm, and appears in the positive arm.

a diagnostic absent from every control golden is an orphan: true
the same diagnostic fragment in a control golden is covered: true
one control marker occurrence covers only one identical diagnostic: true
a scanner absent from the manifest is detected by the population equality: true
repo-relative scanner paths keep equal basenames distinct: true

The rule is put to a tree this repository does not contain: two rules declaring only
variables the ambient record leaves out (`HOME`, a reserved tooling name, `OCANNL_BACKEND`), and
the same tree declaring `(env_var OCANNL_BUILD_FILES_PREFIX)` inside a `(subdir …)` group and
`(env_var ocannl_backedn)` at the top level besides. Nothing else differs between the two runs.

the checker exits 1 and names a key the ambient record carries, declared inside a `(subdir …)` group: true
an unknown lowercase name the record carries too is named in the same run: true
the same tree declaring only someone else's variable, a reserved tooling name and the backend passes and says nothing about it: true

The credential rule is put to the passing tree above plus one rule whose only dependency is
a single `(env_var ...)`: a deny-listed name, a name matched by a pattern, and a near miss;
then to the same rule reading a name through `%{env:...}` instead, to the program's module
reading one by name, and to the near miss again with no deny-list and with one the reader
refuses (gh-ocannl-1280).

a stanza declaring `(env_var GH_TOKEN)`, a name on the deny-list, is refused by name: true
a stanza declaring `(env_var FOO_API_KEY)`, matched by the deny-list's `*_API_KEY`, is refused by name: true
the same tree declaring `(env_var FOO_API_KEYS)`, which no pattern matches, passes: true
a stanza reading `%{env:GH_TOKEN=}`, declaring nothing, is refused by name: true
the same stanza reading `%{env:FOO_API_KEYS=}` passes: true
a module reading `Sys.getenv_opt "GH_TOKEN"`, under the passing tree, is refused by name: true
a run handed no deny-list is refused rather than passing every declaration: true
a deny-list pattern of a shape the reader cannot match like the shell is refused: true

The relationship is put to a tree of one rule, which runs `python3` on a file that is or is
not one this workspace builds. Nothing else differs between the three runs.

an external command handed a file this workspace builds is a stanza the rule reaches, reported by name when it declares neither: true
the same stanza, declaring its backend, passes with the raw-text floor naming it too: true
the same command handed nothing of this workspace is a stanza neither reader sees: true
a local probe declaration is valid with its actual instrumentation owner: true
a transitive library path proves the probe can link instrumentation: true
linking instrumentation alone does not declare lifecycle intent: true
a probe without an instrumentation-capable library is refused: true
an expansion in a marked probe's libraries is refused: true
a select in a marked probe's libraries is explicitly unsupported: true
a marker must name a unit its stanza actually builds: true
an empty unit entry is refused: true
a repeated unit is refused: true
a repeated marker on one stanza is refused: true
a marker between stanzas is refused: true
a marker inside a subdir wrapper but outside its children is refused: true
a marker on a rule is refused: true
a marker on a library without inline tests is refused: true
an inline-test library can declare a lifecycle probe: true
a marker inside a string cannot declare a probe: true
a marker without a reason separator is refused: true
a marker with a one-word reason is refused: true
a marker may select one unit of a plural stanza: true
a marker may select both units of a plural stanza: true
one plural-unit declaration yields exactly that unit's membership: true
a same-named module outside the instrumentation's Dune directory lends no capability: true
removing instrumentation ownership invalidates a probe declaration: true
an opaque dependency path without an established owner is refused: true
a dependency cycle cannot fabricate instrumentation capability: true

The focused-aggregate rule is put to a tree of one member stanza and a family alias that does
or does not list it. Nothing else differs between the runs of a pair; the member takes each of
the shapes dune builds a member in -- a test, an executable with its runner, a plural stanza,
an inline-test library, a `(subdir …)` group -- and the last run is a stanza neither
derivation calls a member.

a stanza whose backend marker names metal is reported, naming its family, when the metal-codegen alias does not reach it: true
the same tree with the member listed in the family stanza passes: true
a stanza declaring a lifecycle probe with instrumentation-capable libraries is reported the same way when the lifecycle alias does not reach it: true
the same tree with that member listed passes too: true
a local declaration remains a member without any OCaml module reference: true
the same declared unit with its family alias listed passes without resolving OCaml: true
an executable whose RUNNER carries the metal marker is a member too, reported when the family does not reach that rule: true
the same tree with the runner's own alias listed passes: true
a plural stanza with only one of its two generated aliases listed is reported, naming the one still missing: true
the same plural stanza with both listed passes: true
the same one-alias listing is COMPLETE when only that unit is declared a lifecycle probe, so the lifecycle family is not asked for its neighbour: true
an inline-test library carrying the metal marker is a member, reported when the family does not reach the alias dune generates for it: true
the same library listed under its generated alias passes: true
a member inside a `(subdir …)` group is aggregated by a family alias in that same group, which the recursive build from the root reaches: true
a runner that runs another directory's executable of the same name does not aggregate this directory's member: true
the same runner running this directory's executable does: true
a runner naming the executable's `(public_name …)` aggregates it as much as one naming its .exe: true
an executable declared in a `(subdir …)` group is aggregated by the top-level family stanza when the rule that runs it sits at the top level: true
a marked rule whose only alias is the directory-wide `runtest` has no focused alias to offer, and a family listing `runtest` does not aggregate it: true
a public name belongs to its own executable, so a rule running the second unit's public name does not aggregate the first: true
a source that names the instrumentation in a comment, a string and a longer identifier reads none of it, and is no member: true
a family alias defined inside a `(subdir …)` group needs that group's own ambient gate, and is reported without one: true
a `(subdir …)` group whose actions take the training lock and whose gate does not is reported there too: true
a rule running a file that shares the executable's public name is not its runner, however alike the two strings are: true
an executable run by a `(test)` stanza's custom action is aggregated through the `runtest-<name>` dune generates for that test: true
two stanzas that omit `(modules …)` get a main each, so only the declared unit is a lifecycle member: true
an absolute path ending in the local executable's name is not the local executable: true
a rule on a `(test)`'s generated alias that runs ANOTHER directory's binary is not the deliberate gate collision, and is reported: true
a rule reusing the alias dune generates for a `(test)` in the same `(subdir …)` group is reported there too: true
a stanza neither derivation calls a member is asked for no family alias: true
declaring a configuration variable the ambient record already carries is refused: true
a tracing dependency without a module reading it is refused: true
an exemption whose read disappears is refused: true
The per-module inline-test aliases (gh-ocannl-1037) are put to a synthetic library of two
tested modules and an untested helper, once wired completely and once broken each way.

a rule per tested module passes unaggregated, and the untested helper is asked for nothing: true
a tested module with no per-module rule is reported, and the checker exits 1: true
a rule whose alias names one module and whose run tests another is reported: true
a per-module alias listed in the `runtest` aggregate is reported as a double run: true

The cc search rule (gh-ocannl-1368) is put to a tree of one `(test)` whose module starts a
search of a stand-in `Autotune`, directly or through a stand-in framework or support wrapper,
beside a stand-in `Fissioned_chain` stating `cc_vector_bytes` and `cc_vector_registers`
the way the small menu does. The arms differ in where the program pins the file, in what its
stanza says, in one renamed entry point, and in what the stand-in helpers state.

a program starting a search with a run that can select cc is reported when it neither pins the width nor carries the marker: true
the same stanza carrying `; cc-vector-width: independent -- <reason>` passes: true
and so does one pinning the variable with `setenv` around its run: true
and so does a program whose initialization calls a support helper stating the width: true
a program's own statements of the keys are no pin: only a blessed helper or a run's is: true
nor does a read of the key, or a helper printing its name: true
a search started through a framework wrapper of the entry point is a search too: true
a program whose only run names a backend other than cc is not asked: true
a marker on a program that starts no search is refused as stale: true
a marker on a program that pins the width is refused as contradictory: true
a marker declaring anything but `independent` is refused: true
two markers on one stanza are refused: true
the marker's sentinel outside a comment is refused: true
a marker on a rule, which names no program's modules, is refused: true
a marker between stanzas is refused rather than attributed: true
an entry point renamed away is refused as a lost anchor, not read as no search: true
a helper that only reads the configuration table pins nothing: true
nor a `setenv` of `-1` or of the empty string, which reads as unset: true
a call through another module's or a local module's namesake of the helper pins nothing: true
while one through an alias of the helper's module does: true
a program pinning the width but not the register count is reported: true
a `setenv` of `-1` around a run undoes an initializer's statement of the width: true
a helper spelling that a later namesake module rebinds is not credited at the rebinding: true
in a plural `(tests)` stanza, one program's pin is not another's, while a program pinning itself passes: true
a helper called after the first search pins nothing: true
nor does one under a command-line `--ocannl_cc_vector_bytes=-1`: true
a search started through a support value reaching the entry point is a search too: true
a command-line setting in another spelling the runtime reads unpins too: true
a helper in a closure the initializer builds but never calls pins nothing: true
nor does one whose key a later item spells again before the first search: true
while a call under a condition, the `if is_cpu` shape, does: true
a bare call under an `open` of the helper's module, which a value can shadow, pins nothing: true
a `setenv` of `-1` around one of a run's invocations undoes the helper's pin: true
a blessed helper that stops stating a key, and a support value stating every key unblessed, are both refused: true

Synthetic controls: ambient gates derive identity from the program actually run.
a renamed direct-utils universe test and its resolved runner are gates: true
a universe-dependent compiler census and backend-free test are not ambient gates: true
a runner of another directory's same-named binary is not an ambient gate: true
a universe rule running an unrelated binary or no program is not an ambient gate: true
a cached runner of the real gate cannot provide an ambient gate: true
only the actual gate's test name receives the generated-alias collision exemption: true
a gate borrowed from another dune file, its public name and an explicit self-run resolve: true
an ordinary direct-utils test and a subtracted linkall flag do not declare a gate: true
a linkall flag restored outside a subtraction is an effective gate declaration: true
preprocessing-only universe dependencies leave force-linked tests cached: true
unresolved flag includes, expansions and subtracted standard sets are refused clearly: true
universe users alone leave their alias entry points ungated: true
a diff reading an output whose rule depends on the gate is gated: true
a diff reading an output whose rule does not depend on the gate, or naming a gated output under no-infer, under chdir or as a plain argument, stays ungated: true

Synthetic controls: escaping source_tree rules must join the scans aggregate.
an unaggregated targetless source_tree scan reports that it inventories the repository: true
an undiffed source_tree output reports that it inventories the repository: true
a repository-wide scan rule running nothing this check can name is refused: true
a repository-wide scan rule running a script rather than an executable is refused: true
a repository-wide scan rule running an executable whose source the check was not handed is refused: true

Synthetic controls: mixed-file gateless exemptions apply only to their named aliases.
an unrelated configuration-reading alias cannot inherit the probe's gateless exemption: true
the original canary and local alias aggregation retain their configuration-free exemption: true
a configuration reader attached to either existing canary alias still requires a gate: true
canary owner linkage or module drift cannot inherit a configuration-free exemption: true
unsupported canary launchers or unresolved alias dependencies cannot claim the exemption: true
each missing scoped exemption is stale even while its sibling alias remains: true
The tracing walk is handed one module that does not parse, and one that parses with a planted
defect in its reader.

a module of an explicit `(modules …)` that does not parse reaches the parse refusal: true
a reader exception on a module that parses reaches the scanner-defect refusal, not the parse one: true

The checker is put to one dune file built to trip the marker grammar and the declaration
checks, and to a run handed nothing at all.

a dune file built to trip the marker grammar and the declaration checks is refused: true
a run handed no dune file and no source is refused: true

